[center][color=#58D1B2][size=5][b]AIM - Alternative Installer for Mistria[/b][/size][/color][/center]
[b][color=#58D1B2][size=4]About AIM[/size][/color][/b]
[color=#F3EEE7]AIM is an independently maintained, open-source mod installer for [b]Fields of Mistria[/b]. It is a fork of MOMI, developed under a separate name with an enhanced user interface and additional compatibility and safety features.[/color]
[color=#F3EEE7]AIM is not intended to replace [url=https://www.nexusmods.com/fieldsofmistria/mods/78]MOMI[/url]. If MOMI eventually provides the features and fixes maintained by AIM, this project may become unnecessary.[/color]
[color=#58D1B2][size=4][b]Features[/b][/size][/color]
[list]
[*][color=#F3EEE7]Self-contained single-executable packages for Windows, Linux/SteamOS, and macOS. No separate .NET installation is required.[/color][/*]
[*][color=#58D1B2]Automatic detection of the Fields of Mistria installation folder.[/color][/*]
[*][color=#B8AEA3]Automatic detection of nearby [b]mods[/b], [b]Mods[/b], or [b]MODS[/b] folders.[/color][/*]
[*][color=#58D1B2]Support for extracted mod folders and ZIP/RAR archives; Nexus downloads can also unpack 7z archives.[/color][/*]
[*][color=#F3EEE7]Profiles for different mod configurations, four optional Mistria-inspired palettes, and remembered text-size controls.[/color][/*]
[*][color=#B8AEA3]Search by mod name, author, description, or version; filtering never changes the saved mod order.[/color][/*]
[*][color=#B8AEA3]Drag-and-drop mod ordering with a visible insertion position and automatic scrolling for long mod lists.[/color][/*]
[*][color=#F3EEE7]Installation and uninstallation with archive backups, validation, and rollback protection.[/color][/*]
[*][color=#C05C46]Compatibility warnings for legacy GML, hooks, seams, shared files, and keyboard shortcuts.[/color][/*]
[*][color=#58D1B2]OAuth-based Nexus integration through the [b]Vortex download button[/b] and [b]nxm://[/b] links, with clear Windows Default Apps guidance when another program remains selected.[/color][/*]
[*][color=#F3EEE7]Automatic monitoring of the selected mods folder.[/color][/*]
[*][color=#B8AEA3]Load-order suggestions plus an on-demand, copyable issue report with exact shared files and compatibility details.[/color][/*]
[*][color=#F3EEE7]Optional direct game launching or Steam launching.[/color][/*]
[*][color=#58D1B2]Fully localized user interface in Bulgarian, German, Spanish, French, Indonesian, Japanese, Korean, Dutch, Polish, Portuguese (Brazil), Russian, Ukrainian, Simplified Chinese, and Traditional Chinese.[/color][/*]
[/list]
[color=#58D1B2][size=4][b]What's New in AIM 0.2.1[/b][/size][/color]
[color=#F3EEE7]AIM 0.2.1 is a focused reliability update for two essential release flows.[/color]
[list]
[*][color=#F6C177][b]Use AIM for NXM now uses the AIM you opened:[/b] after you explicitly confirm the takeover, AIM updates its Windows registration to the exact executable you are running. It still respects a Windows Default Apps choice you made separately.[/color][/*]
[*][color=#58D1B2][b]Release update checks restored:[/b] AIM again finds the newest valid stable GitHub release and shows the existing dismissible update banner. Draft and prerelease tags are ignored.[/color][/*]
[/list]
[color=#58D1B2][size=4][b]AIM 0.2.0 Highlights[/b][/size][/color]
[color=#F3EEE7]AIM 0.2.0 focuses on making a large mod setup easier to read, safer to update, and easier to use in your own language.[/color]
[list]
[*][color=#F3EEE7][b]Complete language coverage:[/b] every shipped language now covers current Nexus, watched-folder, crash diagnosis, conflict research, update, and settings messages. AIM no longer falls back to English halfway through an uncommon flow.[/color][/*]
[*][color=#B8AEA3][b]A responsive mod list:[/b] the old game-detection banner is gone. Search, filters, actions, and status share a toolbar that uses a wide window well but wraps cleanly when the window is compact, leaving more room for mods.[/color][/*]
[*][color=#58D1B2][b]Themes and readable text:[/b] AIM follows your system theme by default. You can also choose Light, Dark, Mistria Harvest, Mistria Meadow, Mistria Night, or Rose Garden from the menu. A-/A+ changes the application text size immediately and remembers your preference.[/color][/*]
[*][color=#F3EEE7][b]Dialogs that fit the screen:[/b] long translated paths and explanations now wrap within consistent dialog bounds instead of creating a window wider than the display.[/color][/*]
[*][color=#F6C177][b]Clear NXM ownership:[/b] registering AIM for nxm:// no longer claims it silently displaced another program. On Windows, AIM explains when a different default remains and offers to open Default Apps so you can choose deliberately.[/color][/*]
[*][color=#58D1B2][b]Safer downloads and updates:[/b] if an update or multi-mod Nexus download fails during extraction, AIM restores the exact previous copy automatically. Retained recovery copies are named clearly if manual help is needed.[/color][/*]
[*][color=#B8AEA3][b]No more accidental duplicate installs:[/b] AIM identifies an already-installed mod by manifest identity rather than only its folder name, asks before replacement, and leaves a hand-downloaded duplicate alone.[/color][/*]
[*][color=#F3EEE7][b]Watched download folders:[/b] choose one or more folders where you download mods by hand. AIM watches them, recognises only mod archives and manifest folders, then moves them into the selected Mods folder and refreshes the list. Unrelated downloads stay exactly where they are.[/color][/*]
[*][color=#B8AEA3][b]Safe watched-folder imports:[/b] when the source and Mods folders are on different drives, AIM copies through a hidden staging folder and only reveals the mod after the full copy succeeds. Interrupted imports leave no half-written mod to install.[/color][/*]
[*][color=#C05C46][b]Check Issues and Find a fix:[/b] AIM reports shared files, validation warnings, duplicate installs, and hotkey clashes in one place. It can search the relevant Nexus pages for compatibility evidence and patches, recognise a patch you already have, enable and order it when appropriate, or record your own verdict.[/color][/*]
[*][color=#F6C177][b]Crash check that keeps its work:[/b] AIM can test suspects one at a time by rebuilding and launching the game, keeps ruled-out and likely-cause verdicts across sessions, turns cleared mods back on, and labels a confirmed or manually marked crasher in the main list. It can also offer safe, removal-only fixes for a few provably broken mod-data lines.[/color][/*]
[*][color=#58D1B2][b]Updates and version history:[/b] clearer update questions and warning badges; per-mod update, freeze, rollback, changelog, Nexus-page, and folder actions. A copy AIM edits or replaces is retained so you can return to an earlier version.[/color][/*]
[*][color=#B8AEA3][b]Load order you can understand:[/b] Suggest order respects declared requirements, considers what each mod installs, preserves your choices inside each safe layer, and explains every proposed move. You can also send a selected mod straight to the top or bottom of the real load order.[/color][/*]
[*][color=#F3EEE7][b]Shortcuts and bindings:[/b] Ctrl+F focuses search, Ctrl+A selects only the currently visible filtered mods, and the Keybinds window shows mod keyboard/controller clashes and can rebind supported settings safely.[/color][/*]
[*][color=#58D1B2][b]Everyday mod management:[/b] improved config editing finds the settings files mods actually use, including MMAPI config stored outside the mod folder.[/color][/*]
[*][color=#F3EEE7][b]Current compatibility work:[/b] refreshed Fields of Mistria 1.0.x/MMAPI data, with validation across old-GML, MMAPI/GML, content-mod, and NXM workflows.[/color][/*]
[/list]
[color=#58D1B2][size=4][b]For Mod Authors and Tool Builders[/b][/size][/color]
[color=#F3EEE7]AIM is also useful while making or testing a mod; these tools are optional and do not change the normal player workflow.[/color]
[list]
[*][color=#F3EEE7][b]Edit the real files:[/b] right-click a mod to open its manifest or its own saved configuration in your normal editor. AIM recognises both [b]manifest.toml[/b] and [b]manifest.json[/b], rather than assuming every mod uses JSON.[/color][/*]
[*][color=#B8AEA3][b]Preflight without touching the game:[/b] [b]AIM-cli --lint[/b] checks one mod; [b]AIM-cli --dry-run --install[/b] runs the normal manifest, seam, skip, and compile checks without rebuilding assets.zip. [b]--doctor[/b] reports whether the game archive and verified backup are safe.[/color][/*]
[*][color=#58D1B2][b]Automation-friendly reports:[/b] list-mods, status, doctor, and dry-run commands can emit JSON or TOML. [b]--seam-check[/b] validates AIM's embedded MMAPI seam catalog against a pristine game archive, with a JSON form for scripts and CI.[/color][/*]
[*][color=#F3EEE7][b]Opt-in in-game debugging:[/b] MMAPI includes a dormant debug agent for mod development. Once enabled in MMAPI's own config, it supports watches, conditional breakpoints, callable debug functions, F9 pause/resume, F10 single-step, and a simple control.json/state.json protocol for external tools.[/color][/*]
[*][color=#58D1B2][b]Current MMAPI extension points:[/b] the shipped catalog is refreshed for Fields of Mistria 1.0.x and includes documented hook support for dialogue prompt locking and font-style loading, alongside the broader hook and seam catalog.[/color][/*]
[*][color=#B8AEA3][b]Reference documentation:[/b] full command-line, manifest, hook, seam, and debugger documentation is maintained in the [url=https://github.com/AcTePuKc/Mods-of-Mistria-Installer/tree/main/docs]AIM documentation on GitHub[/url].[/color][/*]
[/list]
[color=#58D1B2][size=4][b]Installation[/b][/size][/color]
[list=1]
[*][color=#F3EEE7]Download the ZIP archive for your operating system.[/color][/*]
[*][color=#F3EEE7]Extract the archive to any folder of your choice.[/color][/*]
[*][color=#F3EEE7]Start the executable for your system:[/color][/*]
[/list]
[code]Windows: AIM.exe
Linux / SteamOS: AIM-linux
macOS: AIM-osx[/code]
[color=#F3EEE7]AIM will try to detect the game installation and a nearby mods folder automatically. You can also select or change both folders manually from Settings.[/color]
[color=#F6C177][b]Important:[/b] The Nexus ZIP archives contain one self-contained executable. Do not run the executable directly from inside the ZIP archive; extract it first.[/color]
[color=#58D1B2][size=4][b]Adding and Installing Mods[/b][/size][/color]
[list=1]
[*][color=#F3EEE7]Place extracted mod folders or ZIP/RAR archives in your selected mods folder. After OAuth sign-in, the Nexus [b]Vortex download button[/b] ([b]nxm://[/b]) can download and unpack a mod, including a 7z archive.[/color][/*]
[*][color=#F3EEE7]AIM will detect available mods automatically.[/color][/*]
[*][color=#F3EEE7]Select the mods you want to keep active and press [b]Install[/b].[/color][/*]
[*][color=#F3EEE7]Review any compatibility or conflict warnings before continuing.[/color][/*]
[*][color=#F3EEE7]Use [b]Uninstall All[/b] when replacing or testing a different mod set if you want a clean rebuild.[/color][/*]
[/list]
[color=#F3EEE7][b]Downloaded by hand?[/b] Open the gear menu, choose [b]Watched download folders[/b], then [b]Watch a folder...[/b] to bring mods from any download folder into your selected Mods folder automatically.[/color]
[color=#F6C177][b]Language mods:[/b] Language mods often modify shared localization files. AIM warns when selected mods share files. Usually install only one language mod at a time unless you have verified that the selected language mods are compatible.[/color]
[color=#58D1B2][size=4][b]Nexus Integration[/b][/size][/color]
[list]
[*][color=#F3EEE7]AIM uses OAuth PKCE rather than a personal Nexus API key.[/color][/*]
[*][color=#F3EEE7]The GUI can handle account sign-in, Vortex/nxm:// links, downloads, and update checks; AIM CLI does not provide these operations.[/color][/*]
[*][color=#F3EEE7]Free accounts may need the Nexus website's Vortex button for direct update downloads; AIM opens the latest file page when Nexus requires that short-lived website token.[/color][/*]
[*][color=#F3EEE7]Downloaded multi-mod bundles roll back as a unit; retained backup paths are reported if manual recovery is needed.[/color][/*]
[*][color=#F3EEE7]Downloaded archives are limited to 20,000 entries, 512 MiB per entry, and 2 GiB total extracted data; extraction can be cancelled.[/color][/*]
[/list]
[color=#F6C177][b]Note:[/b] AIM uses OAuth PKCE for Nexus account sign-in, Vortex downloads, and update checks. AIM does not request or accept personal Nexus API keys.[/color]
[color=#F6C177][b]Older local installations:[/b] Mods installed before AIM 0.1.5 may not have an exact Nexus file identity recorded. Associate them manually before using update checks.[/color]
[color=#58D1B2][size=4][b]Compatibility[/b][/size][/color]
[list]
[*][color=#F3EEE7]Fields of Mistria 1.0.4 and later 1.0.x versions.[/color][/*]
[*][color=#F3EEE7]Windows x64.[/color][/*]
[*][color=#F3EEE7]Linux x64 / SteamOS x64.[/color][/*]
[*][color=#F3EEE7]macOS x64.[/color][/*]
[/list]
